      "confidence": 0.82,
      "rationale": "Inverted-prior search finds substantial 2024–2025 work on two of the three complications the entry bundles, so the problem is not open as a blank slate. On repeated participation and design-induced dependence, Limozin, Seaman and Su (arXiv 2407.08317; Stat Methods Med Res 2025) run a dedicated simulation comparing sandwich, nonparametric bootstrap, linearised estimating-function bootstrap and jackknife CIs for marginal risk differences under sequential trial emulation (STE), explicitly because the same patient can enter many nested trials. They report that sandwich/bootstrap/jackknife can account for that correlation, that sandwich is conservative when weight uncertainty is ignored, and that LEF bootstrap has the best small/moderate-sample coverage. That is exactly the variance and person-level bootstrap coverage work the agenda’s “direction” asks for; TrialEmulation is the implementation vehicle they use. On calendar-time confounding of pooled contrasts, DeMonte, Shook-Sa and Hudgens (arXiv 2403.18115) formalize nested trial emulation when the effect is allowed to vary over calendar time and/or time since treatment, provide a trial-effect-homogeneity test, and estimate trial-specific as well as calendar-time-varying vaccine effectiveness. That is the “model calendar time as effect modifier and report trial-specific estimates alongside the pooled one” program, at least in the vaccine setting where calendar-time heterogeneity is most obvious. Keogh et al. (Stat Med 2023) already recommended formal tests of trial-by-covariate interactions and compared sequential-trial efficiency to MSM-IPTW. What remains open, and why this is only partial: (1) Limozin evaluates CI coverage and computation, not a definitive redesign verdict that the efficiency case for STE survives person-clustered variance in general applications; sandwich conservatism means naive SEs can still overstate precision relative to person-level resampling. (2) DeMonte’s calendar-time-varying MSMs are developed and simulated for nested vaccine trials; they do not close the problem for arbitrary sustained pharmacoepidemiologic strategies or for routine non-VE sequential emulations that still pool under a common-effect assumption. (3) The third arm (later eligibility as a consequence of earlier untreated survival, so that trials are causally linked and the selection process depends on the outcome model being correct) is not resolved by either paper; no 2024–2026 work found identifies that selection away or shows it is innocuous under weak conditions. Package activity (TrialEmulation, SEQTaRget on CRAN) implements expansion and weighting but does not by itself close the identification/inference gaps. Cited prior work (Caniglia 2023; Su arXiv 2402.12083) illustrates and software-izes the design rather than solving the three complications. Queries that returned no full solution: “sequential trial emulation selection later eligibility untreated survival”; “efficiency gain sequential nested trial person-cluster sandwich vs naive”; general-journal 2024–2026 searches for a single paper closing all three arms together. Net: variance and calendar-time arms are partially methodologically addressed with evaluated tools; the bundled “still open and easy to overlook” framing is still useful but overstates residual openness if it ignores Limozin 2025 and DeMonte 2024.",
      "weakest_true_restatement": null,
      "resolving_work": [
        {
          "locator": "arxiv:2407.08317",
          "title": "Inference procedures in sequential trial emulation with survival outcomes: comparing confidence intervals based on the sandwich variance estimator, bootstrap and jackknife",
          "year": 2025,
          "what_it_resolves": "Derives and simulates person-level sandwich, nonparametric bootstrap, LEF bootstrap and jackknife inference for STE marginal risk differences when the same individual contributes to multiple nested trials; gives practical guidance on which CI method has near-nominal coverage by sample size, event rate and treatment prevalence. Partially closes the repeated-participation variance arm and the agenda’s call to check coverage against bootstrap over persons.",
          "quote": "The sandwich variance estimator, bootstrap or jackknife can be used to obtain a variance estimate of the parameter estimates in an MSM by accounting for correlations induced by the same patient being eligible for multiple trials. ... LEF bootstrap CIs demonstrated the best coverage with small/moderate sample sizes, low event rates and low treatment prevalence, which were the motivating scenarios for STE."
        },
        {
          "locator": "doi:10.1177/09622802251356594",
          "title": "Inference procedures in sequential trial emulation with survival outcomes: comparing confidence intervals based on the sandwich variance estimator, bootstrap and jackknife",
          "year": 2025,
          "what_it_resolves": "Peer-reviewed Statistical Methods in Medical Research version of the same STE variance comparison (PubMed 40631395); same resolving role as arXiv:2407.08317 for the dependence/variance complication.",
          "quote": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "arxiv:2403.18115",
          "title": "Assessing COVID-19 vaccine effectiveness in observational studies via nested trial emulation",
          "year": 2024,
          "what_it_resolves": "Provides NTE IPW estimators and MSMs that allow the effect to vary over calendar time and/or time since treatment, plus a formal test of trial effect homogeneity; estimates trial-specific and calendar-time-varying effects rather than only a pooled common effect. Partially closes the calendar-time confounding / pooled-contrast arm for nested trials (vaccine effectiveness setting).",
          "quote": "Typically, when nested trial emulation (NTE) is employed, effect estimates are pooled across trials to increase statistical efficiency. However, such pooled estimates may lack a clear interpretation when the treatment effect is heterogeneous across trials. ... COVID-19 VE may vary across trials due to calendar-time-specific factors like newly emerging viral strains and pandemic control policies. The TEH assumption can be assessed using a formal hypothesis test."
        }
      ],
      "counter_evidence": [
        {
          "locator": "arxiv:2407.08317",
          "quote": "Despite the increasing popularity of TTE, there is a lack of research on different methods for constructing confidence intervals (CIs) of treatment effects in STE. ... In the more complex setting of STE, less attention appears to be paid to the development and evaluation of CI construction methods."
        },
        {
          "locator": "arxiv:2403.18115",
          "quote": "Often when NTE is used, treatment effect estimates are pooled across the emulated trials to increase statistical efficiency ... Such pooled estimates may lack a clear interpretation when the treatment effect is heterogeneous across trials."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
